+# Mobile WalletConnect deep-linking on Scaffold-ETH 2 / RainbowKit
+
+## (1) Why nothing auto-opened before the patch
+
+The transaction request *did* reach Rainbow — that's why it's sitting there waiting when
+the user switches manually. What failed was **foregrounding the wallet**, and that is a
+separate thing from delivering the request.
+
+WalletConnect v2 is a **relay transport**, not an app switcher. When
+`writeContractAsync` runs, wagmi hands `eth_sendTransaction` to the WalletConnect
+connector, which serializes it and publishes it to the WC relay (a websocket relay
+network). Rainbow, subscribed to that session, receives it in the background. The relay
+does **not** and cannot force the wallet app to the foreground — the dApp is a web page
+in a mobile browser, and no web page can pull another app forward on its own.
+
+Bringing the wallet forward requires a **deep link** (`rainbow://`, or better a universal
+link). And mobile browsers — iOS Safari especially — only allow navigation to an app
+scheme while there is **transient user activation**: a live, recent user gesture (the tap).
+
+Here is the timing problem. The user taps "Stake". `writeContractAsync` is *async*: it
+simulates the call, estimates gas, fetches the nonce, builds the request, then publishes
+it over the relay. All of that is RPC round-trips. By the time the request is actually on
+the wire and RainbowKit/wagmi would fire the redirect, **the user-activation window from
+the tap has expired**, so the browser silently drops the navigation. On the *connect*
+step this doesn't happen, because RainbowKit fires the deep link synchronously inside the
+tap handler from its own modal — it's in the gesture loop. On a *subsequent signing
+request* the redirect is initiated from application code, after async work, outside the
+gesture. Result: request published fine, wallet never foregrounds → "tapping Stake does
+nothing."
+
+## (2) Why the patch made it worse
+
+```js
+window.location.href = "rainbow://";   // line 1 of the handler
+await writeContractAsync(...);         // line 2
+```
+
+Two independent failures, both caused by ordering:
+
+- **Nothing to sign, because the request doesn't exist yet.** The redirect fires *before*
+  `writeContractAsync` has built and published anything. Rainbow opens to its **home
+  screen** (bare `rainbow://` targets the app, not a signing screen) with an empty
+  session queue. There is genuinely nothing waiting.
+- **The request may never get published at all.** Navigating `window.location.href` away
+  begins tearing down / backgrounding the page. Mobile browsers throttle and suspend JS
+  in a backgrounded tab, so the `await writeContractAsync` that was supposed to run *after*
+  the redirect can stall or never complete — and it can interrupt the relay websocket
+  mid-publish. So the transaction never lands onchain.
+
+Before, the request reliably arrived and could at least be found by switching manually.
+The patch trades a foregrounding bug for a **correctness** bug: it races the app-switch
+against request creation and the request loses.
+
+## (3) The correct pattern
+
+Dispatch the request **first**, then foreground the wallet **after** the request is on the
+relay — and only when a deep link is actually appropriate.
+
+```tsx
+import { useAccount } from "wagmi";
+
+const { connector } = useAccount();
+
+async function handleStake() {
+  const isMobile = /iPhone|iPad|iPod|Android/i.test(navigator.userAgent);
+  const isWalletConnect = connector?.id === "walletConnect";
+
+  // Resolve the redirect target from the SESSION, not a hardcode (see below).
+  let target: string | undefined;
+  if (isMobile && isWalletConnect) {
+    const provider: any = await connector!.getProvider();
+    const redirect = provider?.session?.peer?.metadata?.redirect;
+    target = redirect?.universal || redirect?.native; // prefer universal link
+  }
+
+  // 1. Kick off the request FIRST — this is what publishes eth_sendTransaction.
+  const txPromise = writeContractAsync({ /* address, abi, functionName, args */ });
+
+  // 2. Foreground the wallet AFTER the request is on the wire.
+  if (target) {
+    setTimeout(() => { window.location.href = target!; }, 150);
+  }
+
+  await txPromise;
+}
+```
+
+**Ordering.** Request before redirect. The wallet must have something in its queue at the
+moment it comes to the foreground; that only holds if the publish happens first.
+
+**The delay and why its length matters.** `writeContractAsync` isn't synchronous — the
+`eth_sendTransaction` frame only hits the relay after gas estimation / nonce fetch /
+serialization. The delay exists to let that frame land before the app switches.
+- Too short (0 ms — the patch): wallet foregrounds to an empty session. This is exactly
+  the bug above.
+- Too long: you risk exceeding the browser's transient-activation window (~a few seconds
+  on iOS), after which the navigation is blocked; and the UX lags.
+- ~**100–300 ms** is the practical window: long enough for a single websocket publish
+  after local prep, short enough to stay inside user activation. It's a pragmatic
+  heuristic, not a guarantee — on a slow RPC the publish can outrun a fixed delay, which is
+  why modern RainbowKit/wagmi wire the redirect to the *provider's own request/display
+  event* rather than a timer. If you're on a recent RainbowKit + wagmi/WC v2, prefer
+  letting it handle the redirect and don't hand-roll this at all.
+
+**Which scheme to open.** `connector.id === "walletConnect"` for **every** wallet reached
+over WalletConnect — Rainbow, MetaMask, Trust, Zerion, all of them. It tells you the
+*transport*, never the *wallet*. So `rainbow://` is wrong the moment a MetaMask user taps
+Stake. Read the target from the connected session instead:
+`provider.session.peer.metadata.redirect` — the wallet supplies its own `native` and
+`universal` links at connect time. **Prefer `universal`** (an https link like Rainbow's
+`https://rnbwapp.com`): universal links won't dead-end if the app is missing, and iOS
+handles them more reliably than custom schemes.
+
+**When to skip the deep link entirely:**
+- **Not WalletConnect** — `injected` / browser-extension / in-app-browser connectors
+  (MetaMask or Coinbase in-app browser, where the wallet *is* the browser). No app switch
+  exists; navigating away just breaks the page.
+- **Desktop** — extension or QR flow; a deep link does nothing useful and can navigate the
+  user off the dApp.
+- **No redirect metadata in the session** — unknown wallet, no reliable scheme to open;
+  guessing one produces "cannot open" and a dead end.
+- **No transient user activation** — anything not driven by a direct tap (auto-retry,
+  timer-triggered send) will be blocked anyway; don't attempt it.
+
+**Unresolved questions**
+- Which RainbowKit / wagmi versions is the dApp pinned to? If recent, the manual redirect
+  may be redundant (or conflict with the built-in one) and the real fix is to remove the
+  teammate's patch, not replace it.

